WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.730
Twelve pupils, twelve desks.

2
00:00:03.330 --> 00:00:06.250
That’s four hundred and seventy-nine

3
00:00:06.250 --> 00:00:08.210
million different seating plans.

4
00:00:08.930 --> 00:00:11.210
For a class of forty, the number

5
00:00:11.210 --> 00:00:12.810
has forty-eight digits.

6
00:00:13.580 --> 00:00:16.720
So how does anyone find a good plan?

7
00:00:17.870 --> 00:00:21.710
Today: big problems, good answers, and how

8
00:00:21.710 --> 00:00:23.510
to lead a project that uses them.

9
00:00:24.660 --> 00:00:26.520
When there are too many plans to

10
00:00:26.520 --> 00:00:29.220
check, start with any plan and

11
00:00:29.220 --> 00:00:30.800
improve it in small steps.

12
00:00:31.560 --> 00:00:33.940
Swap two pupils; if the plan

13
00:00:33.940 --> 00:00:35.420
gets better, keep the swap.

14
00:00:36.150 --> 00:00:38.230
Repeat until no swap helps.

15
00:00:39.010 --> 00:00:41.610
That’s local search, or hill climbing.

16
00:00:42.920 --> 00:00:45.360
The catch: it can get stuck.

17
00:00:46.100 --> 00:00:49.160
A plan no single swap improves is

18
00:00:49.160 --> 00:00:51.580
only the best nearby, like a

19
00:00:51.580 --> 00:00:53.080
valley that isn’t the lowest.

20
00:00:53.840 --> 00:00:55.860
In the seating lab, hill climbing

21
00:00:55.860 --> 00:00:58.480
stops anywhere from thirty-seven to

22
00:00:58.480 --> 00:01:01.180
forty-seven, and finds thirty-seven

23
00:01:01.180 --> 00:01:02.740
about one run in four.

24
00:01:03.980 --> 00:01:06.140
Simulated annealing accepts some

25
00:01:06.140 --> 00:01:08.900
worse swaps on purpose: often while

26
00:01:08.900 --> 00:01:11.100
it’s hot, rarely as it cools.

27
00:01:11.880 --> 00:01:14.700
Early on, that lets it climb out of a

28
00:01:14.700 --> 00:01:16.960
valley and find a deeper one.

29
00:01:17.470 --> 00:01:18.770
In the lab, it reaches

30
00:01:18.770 --> 00:01:21.150
thirty-seven about two runs in three.

31
00:01:22.380 --> 00:01:24.260
Its rule fits in a few lines.

32
00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:27.380
A worse swap is kept with a chance

33
00:01:27.380 --> 00:01:29.980
that shrinks the worse it is, and

34
00:01:29.980 --> 00:01:31.200
as the temperature falls.

35
00:01:32.570 --> 00:01:34.610
Big problems rarely come with

36
00:01:34.610 --> 00:01:35.670
a proof of the best.

37
00:01:36.410 --> 00:01:39.490
So decide what good enough means before

38
00:01:39.490 --> 00:01:42.430
you start: a time limit, and a gap.

39
00:01:43.060 --> 00:01:45.740
If you know no plan can cost less than

40
00:01:45.740 --> 00:01:48.160
thirty-five and you’ve found thirty-seven,

41
00:01:48.640 --> 00:01:50.060
you’re within about five percent.

42
00:01:51.330 --> 00:01:51.810
Quick check!

43
00:01:52.420 --> 00:01:55.120
Hill climbing stopped at forty-one,

44
00:01:55.600 --> 00:01:58.360
and no single swap improves it.

45
00:01:58.950 --> 00:02:00.850
Is forty-one the best plan?

46
00:02:02.150 --> 00:02:04.230
Not necessarily: it’s

47
00:02:04.230 --> 00:02:05.570
only the best nearby.

48
00:02:06.510 --> 00:02:09.550
Run it again from other starts, or let

49
00:02:09.550 --> 00:02:11.930
annealing escape, and compare the results.

50
00:02:13.230 --> 00:02:15.290
Now the part no solver does:

51
00:02:15.650 --> 00:02:16.730
leading the project.

52
00:02:17.450 --> 00:02:20.130
Start from the problem and the people

53
00:02:20.130 --> 00:02:23.410
it affects, and agree the goal and the

54
00:02:23.410 --> 00:02:24.550
fairness rules with them.

55
00:02:25.120 --> 00:02:28.080
Collect the data, build a small model,

56
00:02:28.480 --> 00:02:30.260
and test it on last year’s plan.

57
00:02:30.820 --> 00:02:34.280
Pilot it, explain it, and decide together.

58
00:02:35.510 --> 00:02:37.570
Keep a person in charge of the final

59
00:02:37.570 --> 00:02:39.310
call, with a way to appeal.

60
00:02:39.960 --> 00:02:41.920
A teacher who can’t come on Fridays

61
00:02:41.920 --> 00:02:44.040
is a rule the data never knew.

62
00:02:44.650 --> 00:02:46.710
And publish how the plan was made.

63
00:02:47.930 --> 00:02:50.030
In the app, seat twelve pupils.

64
00:02:50.820 --> 00:02:53.240
Run hill climbing a few times, then

65
00:02:53.240 --> 00:02:55.600
annealing, and change the starting

66
00:02:55.600 --> 00:02:56.220
temperature.

67
00:02:57.300 --> 00:02:58.060
Your turn.

68
00:02:58.680 --> 00:03:01.760
The exam rota solver has run for an hour.

69
00:03:02.410 --> 00:03:04.390
It’s within two percent of the bound,

70
00:03:04.690 --> 00:03:06.130
and the deadline is tomorrow.

71
00:03:06.710 --> 00:03:07.650
Do you keep it running?

72
00:03:08.960 --> 00:03:11.060
Two percent is good enough for a rota

73
00:03:11.060 --> 00:03:14.280
due tomorrow: stop, check, explain.

74
00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:17.840
Remember: local search improves step

75
00:03:17.840 --> 00:03:20.320
by step, and can get stuck.

76
00:03:21.020 --> 00:03:22.820
Annealing accepts some

77
00:03:22.820 --> 00:03:24.220
worse moves to escape.

78
00:03:24.930 --> 00:03:26.610
Decide good enough in advance,

79
00:03:26.990 --> 00:03:28.370
and keep people in charge.

80
00:03:29.700 --> 00:03:32.060
Try it in class tomorrow: draw the seating

81
00:03:32.060 --> 00:03:34.260
plan on the board, with names on cards.

82
00:03:34.990 --> 00:03:38.210
Pupils swap two cards at a time, keeping a

83
00:03:38.210 --> 00:03:40.350
swap only if the plan gets better.

84
00:03:40.910 --> 00:03:43.470
When they’re stuck, allow one worse

85
00:03:43.470 --> 00:03:45.370
swap, and see if they do better.
